---
/**
 * /why/: the open CFD problems this library addresses, argued from Teschner's survey.
 *
 * Section ids are linked from other pages: algorithms, modelling, mdao, knowledge,
 * counterfactual. The survey's challenge numbers follow Teschner's article.
 */
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { CRATE_URL, STUDIES_URL, VERIFICATION_URL, MACHINE } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Open CFD problems this library addresses | DeepCausality CFD';
const description =
  'Which of the six unsolved CFD problems in Teschner’s survey this library addresses, what it does about each, and the measurement behind each answer.';

const couplingCode = `Coupling::between_steps()
    .then(VibrationalLagStage::new(/* Millikan-White bath */))
    .then(FiniteRateIonizationStage::new(n_tot).with_density_field("n_tot"))
    .then(RegimeClassify::new(l_char, trigger))
    .then(BankSteeredLift::new(rho_ref, cda_over_m, l_over_d))
    .then(TrajectoryNav::new(q_diag, gnss_var, optical_var).with_imu(imu))
    .then(CyberneticCorrect::new(SafetyEnvelope::new(q_max, g_max, bank_max)))
    .build()`;

const regimeLog = `regime -> slip (GNSS-available), Kn=0.07829109848665225
regime -> slip (GNSS-denied), Kn=0.012690837165407727
regime -> continuum (GNSS-denied), Kn=0.00993838892165156
regime -> continuum (GNSS-available), Kn=0.0002551442196046344`;

const precisionCode = `/// Working precision.
pub type FloatType = f64; // or f32, or deep_causality_num::Float106

/// Lift an exact \`f64\` specification into the working precision.
pub fn ft(x: f64) -> FloatType {
    FromPrimitive::from_f64(x).expect("specification lifts into FloatType")
}`;

const studyCode = `let table = CfdFlow::study("weather-dispersion table")
    .cases(model::weather_cases())
    .baseline(model::standard_day)
    .alternate(model::weather_world)
    .ensemble(constants::MC_DRAWS)
    .couple(|case, draw| world::corridor_coupling(model::bias_departure(case.d_temp), draw))
    .march_for(constants::STEPS, world::initial_field)
    .reduce_ensemble(model::world_row)
    .gates(model::weather_gates())
    .verdict()?;`;
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/why/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why</p>
    <h1>Open CFD problems this library addresses</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Teschner’s survey, drawn from the NASA CFD Vision 2030 Study, names six unsolved problems in
      CFD. This library addresses four of them and one the survey omits: forking a running
      simulation.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec wcard-accent" id="counterfactual">
    <p class="tag">Not on the survey’s list</p>
    <h2>A running simulation can pause and fork</h2>

    <p>
      <code>CfdFlow</code> pauses a running simulation when a condition you name becomes true, then
      continues one branch per candidate decision from that state. The survey’s six entries omit
      this. A simulation has been something you configure and run to completion, so changing a
      decision meant editing an input file and paying for the whole trajectory again.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>CfdFlow</code> separates a problem’s description from its execution. A march runs a
      description until a condition becomes true and returns a resumable pause. A campaign forks
      that pause once per candidate and continues each branch in its own world, a run description
      with one declared difference. The branches share the paused state until one writes to it,
      tensor fields, navigation engine and event log included. The branch point is a flow-resolved
      state, not a rerun from initial conditions.
    </p>

    <p>
      Forking parameters gives a sweep. Forking state mid-flight gives the decision the vehicle
      faces, with the flow, the plasma sheath (the ionized gas around the vehicle) and the
      navigation filter as they stand at that instant.
    </p>

    <ul class="nums">
      <li>Fork sharing is structural and checked by a gate (a pass or fail check on a result): branches enter by reference, with no tensor copied at fork time. Continuing a branch costs about the same as an unforked march; coasting is cheaper at 0.66×.</li>
      <li>Bank-angle corridor (the bank angle is the roll that steers the vehicle’s lift sideways): seventeen branches, six coarse and eleven fine, with the best committed mid-descent.</li>
      <li>Plasma retropropulsion (an engine fired against the flow to slow a descent): across the five mid-burn branches, the velocity shed departs a frozen-drag prediction by up to 139.4 m/s. The drag change is the cited Jarvinen-Adams correlation, not the simulated plume.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="src">
      <a href="/fork-or-sweep/">When to fork and when to sweep</a>
      <a href="/blueprints/fork-a-running-simulation/">Fork a running simulation</a>
      <a href="/examples/">Worked examples</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="modelling">
    <p class="tag">Challenge 2: Physical modelling</p>
    <h2>The regime is read from the flow on every step</h2>

    <p>
      The library classifies the flow regime from the evolved state on every step and logs every
      change. A march can stop on a change, so the run finds a regime change; nobody tells it where
      to switch. The classification does not switch closures: the crate implements closures for the
      continuum band only.
    </p>

    <p>
      The freestream Knudsen number (the ratio of the molecular mean free path to a length scale)
      bands the flow into continuum Navier–Stokes, slip-corrected continuum, transitional or
      free-molecular. The evolved electron density sets the plasma frequency, which decides whether
      the GNSS link (satellite navigation) exists and so which measurements the navigation filter
      folds in. The retropropulsion descent also bands the Mach number and tracks the thrust state.
    </p>

    <p>
      A second criterion, the force ratio <code>ε = a_aero/a_grav</code>, marks where a trajectory
      should change integrator: the exact KS-conformal core with aero as a between-step kick while
      gravity dominates, then direct Cowell integration once aero does. That switch is public API
      (<code>RegimeSwitch</code>) the shipped engine does not call.
    </p>

    <p>The four regime entries in the event log of a committed corridor run:</p>

    <Code code={regimeLog} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      One descent passes through slip flow, continuum flow, comms blackout and reacquisition in one
      uninterrupted program. The log shows one Knudsen band crossing, slip to continuum, and the
      link going denied and returning. Turbulence closures are staged on the discrete-exterior-calculus
      (DEC) solver behind the spectra observables; the <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a> carries the
      sequencing.
    </p>

    <ul class="nums">
      <li>RAM-C II (a reentry flight experiment) stagnation streamline, ~71 km, Mach 25: the uncalibrated finite-rate network, run as prediction rather than fit, lands at 2.251e19 m⁻³ against the ~1e19 m⁻³ flight anchor, +0.35 decades (a decade is a factor of ten) inside a ±0.70 band.</li>
      <li>The closed-form Park-2T controller (a two-temperature relaxation model) lands at 5.31e17 m⁻³, 1.27 decades below the anchor after the N₂–N₂ reduced-mass correction; the offset is reported, not tuned away.</li>
      <li>Plasma frequency ω_p 4.111e10 rad/s against the 9.40e9 rad/s comms band; blackout classified true.</li>
      <li>Validated incompressible envelope Re 100 to 1600; the wake is reported and never gated.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="quote">
      Teschner’s survey, challenge 2: closures are calibrated over a range of flows, then fail
      outside it, and the failure is invisible from inside the run.
    </p>

    <p class="src">
      <a href="/validation/">Validation records</a>
      <a href="/boundaries/">Capability boundaries</a>
      <a href="/boundaries/#regime-diagnostic">Regime classification is a diagnostic</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="algorithms">
    <p class="tag">Challenge 3: Numerical algorithms</p>
    <h2>Error is attributed by precision, and cost falls out of the representation</h2>

    <p>
      The library attributes error by running one program at several precisions. It cuts cost in
      the representation of the flow. Every theory, solver, stage and observable is generic over a
      single real scalar. A program fixes one alias and the whole computation runs at that
      precision, from the flux stencils through to the Kalman filter, downcasting to
      <code>f64</code> only at the display boundary.
    </p>

    <Code code={precisionCode} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      That turns error attribution into an experiment. The turbulence example runs one Lorenz rate
      field at all three precisions in a single program and reports what each buys. The forecast
      horizon of a chaotic flow lands at t ≈ 21.5, 44.5 and about 81 for <code>f32</code>,
      <code>f64</code> and <code>Float106</code>, so round-off, not the step size, caps the
      prediction.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-warn">
      <p class="callout-title">Limit: the 106-bit corridor result is a record</p>
      <p>
        The plasma-blackout corridor was flown the same way once, at <code>f64</code> and again at
        106-bit. Every gate and every discrete event step came out identical, with the continuous
        witnesses agreeing to 15 or 16 significant digits. That result stands as history: it was
        recorded on the superseded surrogate-era build, and re-flying the corridor at 106-bit today
        fails to compile, because the example has accumulated <code>f64</code>-specific code and a
        few spots in the crate have too. Closing that gap would make the corridor’s round-off
        budget a claim rather than a record.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Cost is cut at the representation. The compressible marchers store the flow in a compressed
      quantized form (see <a href="/solvers/">Solvers</a>). A <code>2^L</code> grid
      costs order <code>χ² · L</code>, where χ is the rank of the compression: logarithmic in point
      count, with sharp structure paid for in rank. The rank studies located the cost driver as
      coordinate alignment. The compressible carrier answers it with a shock-fitted inflow strip, so
      the exact Rankine–Hugoniot state bounds the marched layer and the shock is never captured.
    </p>

    <ul class="nums">
      <li>Graded-torus MMS (method of manufactured solutions), 8² → 64²: observed order 1.98–2.01 against 2.00; divergence-freeness exact by construction.</li>
      <li>Taylor–Green RHS kernel against exact autodiff derivatives: max abs error 1.11e-16.</li>
      <li>Precision ladder on the same program: 3e-8 (f32) / 1e-16 (f64) / 8e-33 (Float106).</li>
      <li>Sod shock tube at 512 cells, t = 0.2: density L1 0.0175, velocity 0.0274, pressure 0.0151, all against a 0.03 tolerance; star-region pressure 0.3032 against the canonical p* = 0.3031.</li>
      <li>Taylor–Green in the compressed form at 32²: bond 32 (the rank) against 1024 dense, a 32× compression, observed order 2.18.</li>
      <li>Blunt-body bow shock, 2⁵ → 2⁷: fitted bond 3 → 5, Cartesian capture bond 16 → 61.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="quote">
      Teschner’s survey, challenge 3: uncertainty quantification is “unexplored in most cases”, and
      modelling, boundary-condition and numerical error resist being quantified together.
    </p>

    <p class="src">
      <a href={VERIFICATION_URL}>verification/ <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      <a href={STUDIES_URL}>studies/ <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="knowledge">
    <p class="tag">Challenge 5: Knowledge extraction</p>
    <h2>The run returns a verdict, not a field</h2>

    <p>
      The study grammar reduces inside the process. A campaign names its cases, marches or forks
      them, reduces each outcome to a scored row, and gates the table. The table and the verdict
      cross the process boundary; the field stays where it was computed.
    </p>

    <Code code={studyCode} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      <code>verdict()</code> returns data. The DSL never prints and never exits, so the caller
      decides what an exit code means. The gating sequence is a named value the study inserts whole,
      which makes an acceptance criterion a reviewable object rather than a comment in a
      post-processing script. Every branch stamps a <code>!!ContextAlternation!!</code> marker into
      its event log that names its baseline. On the <code>march_for</code> path, an optional
      <code>save_log(path)</code> flushes the log to disk, one file per branch under a fan-out; fork
      branches keep the log in the returned report.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same discipline covers the crate’s own evidence. <code>verification/</code> holds runnable
      programs gated against analytic solutions, published references, or invariants the
      discretization must preserve. <code>studies/</code> holds the empirical probes that settled
      design questions, with each finding encoded as a gate so the conclusion stays reproducible
      instead of surviving as folklore. Three hypotheses were refuted this way and are recorded as
      refuted.
    </p>

    <ul class="nums">
      <li>Thirteen gated verification programs on the validation page, each with a committed run artifact and a stated caveat.</li>
      <li>Findings encoded as gates: a study that stops reproducing fails, rather than quietly ageing.</li>
      <li>Three refuted hypotheses kept on the record with the measurement that overturned each.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="quote">
      Teschner’s survey, challenge 5: pulling whole simulations onto a workstation stops being
      possible at scale, and extracting actionable information from single runs and parameter
      sweeps needs better frameworks than the post-processor.
    </p>

    <p class="src">
      <a href="/validation/">Validation records</a>
      <a href="/boundaries/">Capability boundaries</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="mdao">
    <p class="tag">Challenge 6: Multidisciplinary analysis and optimization</p>
    <h2>Nothing is exchanged, because nothing is separate</h2>

    <p>
      A coupling here is a compile-time list of physics stages stepping one shared field. The
      disciplines never ran in separate processes, so no file, no adapter and no exchange format
      exist.
    </p>

    <Code code={couplingCode} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      One loop body holds vibrational relaxation, reacting plasma chemistry, regime classification,
      steered aerodynamic force, a 17-state error-state Kalman navigation engine, and a
      bounded-correction safety envelope. Stages communicate through named fields on the evolved
      state, and an <code>Err</code> from any stage short-circuits the whole step.
    </p>

    <p>
      Uncertainty crosses the chain the same way. The ensemble index threads into the stack, so a
      Monte-Carlo draw is a parameter of the coupled physics rather than a wrapper around it. The
      evolved electron density gates which measurements the Kalman filter may fold. The safety
      envelope’s clamped bank command is flown by the aero stage and steers the trajectory that sets
      the next step’s freestream. Fluid dynamics, estimation and control close one loop.
    </p>

    <ul class="nums">
      <li>Plasma retropropulsion descent, blackout exit to touchdown: sixteen gates in 337.5 s on {MACHINE}.</li>
      <li>Weather-dispersion table: six atmospheres declared as differences from one baseline, each an ensemble of receiver-noise draws, flown concurrently to one gated table.</li>
      <li>Branch fan-outs run on scoped threads and produce bits identical to the sequential run.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="quote">
      Teschner’s survey, challenge 6: coupled solvers need a framework, uncertainty propagation
      across a solver chain is unaddressed, and no multidisciplinary exchange format exists
      comparable to CGNS for CFD alone.
    </p>

    <p class="src">
      <a href="/examples/">Worked examples</a>
      <a href={CRATE_URL}>deep_causality_cfd/ <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </p>
  </section>

